        <OtherAbstract Type="PIP" Language="eng">
            <AbstractText>Your correspondent A. G. Mathew (Journal, July 14) has touched on a number of aspects of the IUD which are well-known to workers in the field but which need constant reiteration. IUDs in nulliparous women are imperfect and hazardous for several reasons. The nulliparous woman is usually young and for this reason, the pregnancy rate is higher. This fact, incidentally, was pointed out many years ago in the Journal by A. M. Hill whose obituary appeared in the above-mentioned issue. The symptoms of menorrhagia and dysmenorrhea increase in frequency with IUD use as does the expulsion rate. Pelvic infection with its possible sequelae of infertility, is an iatrogenic complication. Occasionally the added hazard of anesthesia is required for insertion. I have always been skeptical of the theory that the copper content of a device adds anything to its efficacy. All in all, IUDs in nulliparae are undesirable. However, if the patient is made fully aware of the possible consequences, there is no reason that she should not have one. After all, smoking and alcohol are condoned.</AbstractText>
        </OtherAbstract>
